  "public_messages": [
    "I\u2019ll read the brief, notes, and current implementation first, then extend the existing transaction paths and verify the migration and concurrency behavior with the supplied tests. I\u2019ll use the installed OpenSocrates guidance for judgment calls; the implementation work itself is mechanical.",
    "The existing service already routes single and batch submissions through one transaction, and claims and lifecycle updates through shared write transactions. I\u2019ll extend those paths. Migration needs a read-only version check before any journal-setting write so a future-version database stays untouched.",
    "The first test run reached the retry boundary: older lifecycle tests expect immediate retry, while new jobs now default to a 1000 ms delay. I\u2019ll set those test jobs to an explicit zero retry base, then add focused tests for the new default and deadline behavior.",
    "The focused tests now cover admission at capacity, priority with a future job, FIFO ties, cancellation fencing, pagination during insertion, retry boundaries, and a version 1 schema migration. I\u2019m moving to a loopback check with a database created by the preceding executable and two new server processes.",
    "The first simultaneous migration attempt exposed a startup lock while reading `user_version`. I added a bounded retry for that read. The loopback run now passes with data created by the version 1 executable: two new processes open the database, replay the old key, keep the lease valid, retain a completion result, and enforce a shared pending limit.",
    "I\u2019ve updated the README and bounded project notes. I\u2019ll finish by checking simultaneous claims against the inflight limit across two processes, then rerun the full test suite and confirm the supplied module and driver files remain unchanged.",
    "Implemented the version 2 backend in [service.go](<WORKSPACE>/internal/queueforge/service.go:60) and added the admission flags in [main.go](<WORKSPACE>/cmd/server/main.go). Migration preserves version 1 jobs, leases, results, and idempotency; startup rejects future schema versions before changing journal mode.\n\nSingle and batch submissions still use the same transaction path ([submit](<WORKSPACE>/internal/queueforge/service.go:348)). Claims and lifecycle writes retain transactional fencing while adding priority, scheduled availability, retry backoff, quotas, and cancellation. Listing uses a creation-sequence cursor. The new indexes support claim order, inflight and pending counts, and queue-filtered listing; availability is checked separately so a future high-priority job cannot block an eligible job. The API and limits are documented in [README.md](<WORKSPACE>/README.md).\n\n**Verification passed:** `go test ./...`, `go test -race ./...`, and `git diff --check` on Go 1.26.3. [Follow-up tests](<WORKSPACE>/internal/queueforge/followup_test.go) cover retry boundaries, priority and FIFO order, cancellation races, batch rollback, pagination during insertion, migration, and future-version byte preservation. A loopback check used the preceding executable to create real version 1 data, then verified replay, lease and completion continuity, and pending and inflight contention across two new processes. Its cleanup stopped the owned processes; direct process-table inspection was unavailable in this sandbox.\n\nThe supplied driver files and exact `go.mod`/`go.sum` remain unchanged. No commit was made. [PROJECT_NOTES.md](<WORKSPACE>/PROJECT_NOTES.md) records bounded progress. The service still does not execute payloads; attempts are at least once, with fenced completion."
  ],
